					"emailString" : "<unknown>"
				},
				"createTime" : {
					"__type" : "time",
					"time" : {
						"__type" : "dateAndTime",
						"dateAndTimeString" : "2026-09-26T21:49:16.267433+02:00"
					}
				},
				"editEmail" : {
					"__type" : "email",
					"emailString" : "<unknown>"
				},
				"editTime" : {
					"__type" : "time",
					"time" : {
						"__type" : "dateAndTime",
						"dateAndTimeString" : "2026-09-26T21:49:16.267433+02:00"
					}
				},
				"uid" : {
					"__type" : "uid",
					"uidString" : "o6G7A03iSTadfNY1PAIugA=="
				},
				"paragraphStyle" : {
					"__type" : "textStyle"
				},
				"string" : "The simplest server we could write compiles every cell from scratch: the definitions of all previous cells, plus the new one, through the whole compiler. That is correct, but it is also slow, because Carp’s standard library comes along for the ride every single time."
			},
			{
				"__type" : "textSnippet",
				"children" : {
					"__type" : "snippets",
					"items" : [ ]
				},
				"createEmail" : {
					"__type" : "email",
					"emailString" : "<unknown>"
				},
				"createTime" : {
					"__type" : "time",
					"time" : {
						"__type" : "dateAndTime",
						"dateAndTimeString" : "2026-09-26T21:49:16.267433+02:00"
					}
				},
				"editEmail" : {
					"__type" : "email",
					"emailString" : "<unknown>"
				},
				"editTime" : {
					"__type" : "time",
					"time" : {
						"__type" : "dateAndTime",
						"dateAndTimeString" : "2026-09-26T21:49:16.267433+02:00"
					}
				},
				"uid" : {
					"__type" : "uid",
					"uidString" : "DYN3PQ2ITDaTttWuDVCK5w=="
				},
				"paragraphStyle" : {
					"__type" : "textStyle"
				},
				"string" : "A warm session keeps the compiler’s state around instead. When a session starts, the server loads and infers core once. After that, a cell goes through the compiler’s front end on its own, checked against what is already there, and its definitions are absorbed into the session so the next cell can see them. For small snippets, compiling becomes a matter of milliseconds!"
			},
			{
				"__type" : "textSnippet",
				"children" : {
					"__type" : "snippets",
					"items" : [ ]
				},
				"createEmail" : {
					"__type" : "email",
					"emailString" : "<unknown>"
				},
				"createTime" : {
					"__type" : "time",
					"time" : {
						"__type" : "dateAndTime",
						"dateAndTimeString" : "2026-09-26T21:49:16.267433+02:00"
					}
				},
				"editEmail" : {
					"__type" : "email",
					"emailString" : "<unknown>"
				},
				"editTime" : {
					"__type" : "time",
					"time" : {
						"__type" : "dateAndTime",
						"dateAndTimeString" : "2026-09-26T21:49:16.267433+02:00"
					}
				},
				"uid" : {
					"__type" : "uid",
					"uidString" : "l2XET2gLQa2Pq60sBBqJng=="
				},
				"paragraphStyle" : {
					"__type" : "textStyle"
				},
				"string" : "A project session works the same way, except that its base is the whole project rather than just core. It takes a little longer to load, but then every cell runs against everything the project defines. On the GT side, that starts in {{gtMethod:CarpProject>>#loadSession}}."
			},
			{
				"__type" : "textSnippet",
				"children" : {
					"__type" : "snippets",
					"items" : [ ]
				},
				"createEmail" : {
					"__type" : "email",
					"emailString" : "<unknown>"
				},
				"createTime" : {
					"__type" : "time",
					"time" : {
						"__type" : "dateAndTime",
						"dateAndTimeString" : "2026-09-26T21:49:16.267433+02:00"
					}
				},
				"editEmail" : {
					"__type" : "email",
					"emailString" : "<unknown>"
				},
				"editTime" : {
					"__type" : "time",
					"time" : {
						"__type" : "dateAndTime",
						"dateAndTimeString" : "2026-09-26T21:49:16.267433+02:00"
					}
				},
				"uid" : {
					"__type" : "uid",
					"uidString" : "f2YhHQ5GQv2stnuB8mj+iw=="
				},
				"paragraphStyle" : {
					"__type" : "textStyle"
				},
				"string" : "The tricky part is getting everyone to agree. One session answers evaluation, types, completion, and documentation, and those answers come from different places inside the server. If a definition works when we run it, completion should offer it, and the type annotation shouldn’t call it unknown. When the server can’t keep its warm state in step, it falls back to slower paths rather than answer from state it knows is wrong. If you want the gory details, `INVARIANTS.md` in the repository maps which answer comes from where, and which tests keep an eye on them."
			},
			{
				"__type" : "textSnippet",
				"children" : {
					"__type" : "snippets",
					"items" : [ ]
				},
				"createEmail" : {
					"__type" : "email",
					"emailString" : "<unknown>"
				},
				"createTime" : {
					"__type" : "time",
					"time" : {
						"__type" : "dateAndTime",
						"dateAndTimeString" : "2026-09-26T21:49:16.267433+02:00"
					}
				},
				"editEmail" : {
					"__type" : "email",
					"emailString" : "<unknown>"
				},
				"editTime" : {
					"__type" : "time",
					"time" : {
						"__type" : "dateAndTime",
						"dateAndTimeString" : "2026-09-26T21:49:16.267433+02:00"
					}
				},
				"uid" : {
					"__type" : "uid",
					"uidString" : "B0hWsmbXQumszQrPI6QZ0Q=="
				},
				"paragraphStyle" : {
					"__type" : "textStyle"
				},
				"string" : "If you are adding your own language, this is the part I’d steal. Being able to ask a compiler more than one question about the same program, cheaply, is what everything else in this part is built on."
			}
		]
	},
